-- title:
--   Every distance between distinct lattice points has a dyadic-type scale
-- statement:
--   Let $\Delta > 1$ be a real number and let $x \ne y$ be two points of the integer lattice $\mathbb{Z}^d$. Then there is a natural number $m$ with
--
--   $$\Delta^{m} \;\le\; \lVert x - y\rVert \;<\; \Delta^{m+1}.$$
--
--   The exponent $m$ is the *logarithmic scale* of the pair $(x,y)$ at base $\Delta$. That $m$ may be taken to be a natural number, rather than an arbitrary integer, is exactly the content of the lattice hypothesis: distinct lattice points are at distance at least $1 = \Delta^0$, so the scale is never negative.
--
--   The statement is the indexing device for any construction that treats a pair of lattice points at a scale adapted to their separation: it assigns to each pair a single well-defined nonnegative scale, so that a family of local constructions indexed by $m \in \mathbb{N}$ suffices to handle all pairs.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $\Delta$ is real, not an integer, and $m$ ranges over $\mathbb{N}$; the hypotheses $x, y \in \mathbb{Z}^d$ and $x \ne y$ are both used, the first to obtain $\lVert x-y\rVert \ge 1$ and the second to exclude $\lVert x-y\rVert = 0$.
